The UDC provides for the annual review process whichis a process intended to refine the
existing codes and is structured to provide for some of the discussion andvetting described
above.During the most recent annual review process a number of modifications were presented.
Staff has completed the first set and begun work on the others.One of the others which is in
process relates to Councilman Shanor's more targeted landscape reduction focused on building
reuse which the Planning Commission endorsed with additional input.Although work is
substantially underway to address this work item, work is delayed at this point in order to
process this ordinance for Councilman Shanor which is a substantially larger policy change not
discussed during the annual review process. Other recommended items also on hold include:
election signs, coordinated approvals processing for new development and documenting PUD's
and overlay districts appropriately.
Proposal
The proposal is to reduce the landscape point requirement in half across all developments and to
reduce coverage requirement of the remaining points by a third from 75% to 50%. This will
impact all new developments and redevelopment projects.
Staff received the ordinance from Councilman Shan or via email on June26,2013with
instructions to place the item on the Planning Commission agenda for July 15, 2013.
Councilman Shanor was informed that this was not enough time for legal advertisement and
ordinanceis also sponsored by Councilmen Cook and Allen.
A legal advertisement was place in the newspaper to advertise the public hearing.Planning
Commission will need to act on this item which mayinclude recommendations to approved,
deny, amend or postpone to a meeting occurring within 60 days.
Impacts and considerations
·The proposed reduction of landscape requirements will clearly result in less landscaping
reducing thecost of development and maintenance by the landowners.
·The proposed landscape reduction is likely to result in a far greater predominance of
crushed rock and gravel for portions of a site not covered by buildings or parking.The
current requirement is calibrated to the maximum coverage requirements so that the
landscape point requirement closely matches up with the areas of the site not covered by
buildings and parking. The current requirement leaves the door open for up to 40% of
the non-built site to be void of landscaping. The proposed amendment would allow up to
