A 50% reduction of the landscape points will not necessarily equate to a 50% reduction
in landscape construction costs or in maintenance.Developers will still need to install automatic
irrigation systems that will include the expense of an irrigation tap, back flow prevention device
and possibly even a pump.The reduction of trees won’t substantially reduce the quantity of drip
irrigation that is necessary.Property coverage will remain the same and therefore, the ground
not covered by buildings, parking areas or outside storage will still require some sort of
landscape covering. This will likely occur with what is considered “landscape rock”and it is my
I have heard the comment that landscaping in the City is not appropriate because it is not
native to the area.My answer to this is that neither are lots of people, buildings, parking lots
and/orroadways native to the area.Landscaping effectively and relatively inexpensively
mitigates theimpacts of development.Large trees shade buildings and paved areas, plants and
grasses slow and clean storm-water runoff, landscaping reduces heating and cooling
requirements and cleans the air we breathe. Carefully designed landscaping can improve the
look of less than attractive buildings as well as fine architecture.Landscaping improves the
image of businesses, cities and regions. Most landscape projects cost in the realm of 1-10% of
the total cost of a project.
Ihave heard the comment that trees are a liability.I would agree that a great big, weak
wooded and shallow rooted tree species, planted in a fourfoot by fourfoot treewell or narrow
tree lawn does, over time can become a liability. The roots; as they are reaching for water and
oxygen break surrounding infrastructure such as sidewalks, curbs and gutter.We should indeed,
avoid landscape scenarios like thisbut, overall, a carefully designed and installed landscape,
usingplant species appropriate to the space requires very little maintenance andmost certainly
does not pose health and safety concerns.
Landscapes do require maintenance but I have to ask, what doesn't?You have to replace
roofs, paint buildings, re-pave and re-stripe parking lots, maintain detention areas, wash the
windows, do the dishes, mop and vacuum floors.There is nothing about property ownership that
does not require maintenance.
