E.Detached sidewalk shall only be required where the improvements can be placed
within the public ROW and not on private property.
I don't have an answer to the issue of redevelopment.I doknow that it's a challenging
issue that the City really needs to address.What Ifear is that webegin to just lower the
expectations on all development.This would not serve the citizens of Cheyenne well.
In regards to useful changes for the existing and well established landscape points
system, the following are my recommendations;
1.We should want as many large trees as we can get for the City. They offer the most bang
in terms of cooling, air quality, water quality, aesthetics, etc.But, big trees requirebig
spaces.
2.Increase street tree placement to maximum 50’ spacing. An overall 20% decrease in
4
```

### Page 16 of 24 (scanned page: machine OCR, may contain errors)

```text
planted here.Use of ornamental trees should only be allowed where overhead utilities
conflict and should require appropriate(closer) spacing.
3.Increase point value for larger deciduous trees, specifically trees with a canopy greater
than 40'and now valued at 1.25 points should be revalued to1.75 points. Ornamental
tree values should not increase.While ornamental trees can be very attractive they don't
provide much in terms of shading, cooling, etc.
4.Increase points value for large species evergreens. (Evergreens that have a mature spread
of 25'or greater) Currently valued at 1.25 points each should be revalued at 1.75 points.
Smaller evergreens(<25’mature spread) should remain at1.25 points.
5.Don't change the 75% living groundcover requirement; I think this a reasonable request
and it serves tokeep developers from covering large expanses of their property with rock
or“zero-scape”, which, in my opinion is not landscaping(we might as well let them pave
it)I think that where designers get in trouble here is that they use an inappropriate species
(a shrub that doesn't have a large spreading habit at maturity) or they graphically depict
the plant material with an inappropriate spread.They wouldn't have to use so many
plants if they designed shrubs borders properly. Shrub borders are generally the least
maintenance and use theleast amount of water of all landscape applications.A reduction
from 75%to50%is relatively meaningless when areas like this are designed properly.
6.Irrigation; in an effort to keep development sustainable, consider reducing irrigation
requirements for land located within light and heavy industrially zoned areas(other zone
districts?) In these districts, developers should irrigate all tree and shrub areas with
automatic drip irrigation and tree lawns should be allowed tobe planted with dry-land,
native grasses that are not irrigated.Storey Blvd.is a good example of non-irrigated tree
lawn that does very well.Iunderstand that turf grass (bluegrass or fescue) tree lawns
lookbetter toasociety that prefers the green golf course aesthetic, but they are a
tremendous waste of water.They are very expensive to irrigate and really don't serve
any functional purpose. They also require a lot of inputs, such as regular mowing and
fertilization.Fertilization along roadways can be very problematic to natural waterways
that receive it by way of storm sewers.In general, I believe turf grasses (bluegrasses and
fescues) should be used sparingly in play areas, athletic fields, etc.(essentially, places
that can be used). Tree lawns, while serving as a separator from road traffic and
typically, long narrow areas, do not need to be turf grass. This would be a very water
wise(xeric)addition/changeto the code.
